Put every extension in the one process a single-process phone has.

With extension pages committing on a phone, uBlock Origin Lite's popup
came up bare: its own five stylesheets unreadable (SecurityError), a
fetch of its own css failing, no icon font, the script stuck in
"loading", and chrome.runtime.sendMessage to its service worker never
answered.

ProcessMap records one extension per renderer process. A phone runs
single-process -- one renderer, inside the browser, holding every page
and every extension -- so whichever extension got there first owned the
process, and every other one was "not in its process". Its page's own
subresources were then cross-renderer loads and refused
(AllowExtensionResourceLoad), and its API context was classified the way
a web page's would be.

On OHOS in single-process mode ProcessMap::Contains now answers that
every extension is in the one process there is. That is the process
check only: whether a resource belongs to the extension, whether it is
web-accessible, and the rest of AllowExtensionResourceLoad run as before.
A refused extension resource is also logged in a release build, with the
URL, process and destination -- in the page it is only a failed fetch.

That made every context an extension had on a web page look like the
extension's own page to the browser: GetMostLikelyContextType asks the
same question and got the same answer, so a content script was taken for
a privileged extension page. In single-process mode a context on an
http(s) URL is now a content script, whatever Contains() says; the
extension's own pages are on its own origin and are not affected.

Tablets and 2in1s run renderers in processes of their own and none of
this applies to them.

Generated for Chromium 154.0.8037.51 after
ohos-extension-context-diagnostics.patch.

diff --git a/extensions/browser/extension_protocols.cc b/extensions/browser/extension_protocols.cc
--- a/extensions/browser/extension_protocols.cc
+++ b/extensions/browser/extension_protocols.cc
@@ -279,6 +279,13 @@ bool AllowExtensionResourceLoad(const network::ResourceRequest& request,
     return true;
   }
 
+#if BUILDFLAG(IS_OHOS)
+  // Said in a release build: a refused load is otherwise only a failed
+  // fetch or an unreadable stylesheet in the page.
+  LOG(ERROR) << "OHOS extension resource refused: " << request.url.spec()
+             << " from process " << child_id.GetUnsafeValue()
+             << ", destination " << static_cast<int>(destination);
+#endif
   // No special exceptions for cross-process loading. Block the load.
   return false;
 }
